see code example below.

## Explicit Type Casting

We can manually change an expression to be of a specific type by using a **cast**.

Syntax:

| (type) expression |
| :--- |

where *type *is a valid data type.

For example, to calculate half of **x **(where x is an **int**), we write

| (float) x/2 |
| :--- |

**Cast **is a unary operator and has same precedence as other unary operators.

Code Examples:

```c
// example type conversion

#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
     char c = 'c'; /* ascii value of c is 99 */
     int i = 5;
     float f =7.7f;
     double d = 3.12334;

     /*let we print the expression
     ((c / i) + (f*d) - (f + i)) in different formats
     */
     printf("%d", ((c / i) + (f*d) - (f + i)));
     printf("\n");
     printf("%f", ((c / i) + (f*d) - (f + i)));

     getchar();
     return 0;
}

/* output

396452324
30.349718
*/

/*
Here in the expression ((c / i) + (f*d) - (f + i)),
(c/i) converted into int, (f*d) converted in to double
& (f+i) converted in float. The result of int, double &
float is double and so the final result is in double.
*/
```

